<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel Ney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Marshal Ney</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel Ney was a French soldier and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s marshals, created Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elchingen">Elchingen</a> in 1808 for his victory there in 1805 and later Prince of the Moskva for his part at Borodino. Famed for his courage in commanding the rearguard on the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1812, he rallied to Napoleon during the Hundred Days and led the cavalry charges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>. He was tried for treason and shot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in December 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-ney.jpg" alt="Michel Ney" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Gérard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marechal_Ney.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
